<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cape Town</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1652">1652</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cape Town is a port city on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/table-bay">Table Bay</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. It was founded in 1652 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-van-riebeeck">Jan van Riebeeck</a> as a supply station of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> and grew into the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and then British rule. Since 1910 it has been the legislative capital of South Africa.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cape-town.jpg" alt="Cape Town" width="320" height="185" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Der Berzerker from Washington, DC, USA · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Camps_bay_(53460319478)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Harold Macmillan's “Wind of Change” speech in <b>Cape Town</b> in 1960 acknowledged the inevitability of African decolonisation, and the wave of independence that followed, beginning with Ghana in 1957 and Nigeria in 1960, ended Britain's African empire within little more than a decade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Cape Town</b> grew into a cosmopolitan port with Muslim, Christian and Jewish communities, and the South African College, founded in 1829, became the University of Cape Town.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic kept the Union’s territory, its three capitals of Pretoria, <b>Cape Town</b> and Bloemfontein, and its rule over South West Africa, which the United Nations later declared illegal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Then the gold of the Witwatersrand shifted the economic centre of South Africa from the Cape to the Transvaal, drew railways from <b>Cape Town</b>, Durban and Delagoa Bay to the Rand, and created Johannesburg, which became the country’s largest city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-african-republic">South African Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Union had three capitals: Pretoria for the government, <b>Cape Town</b> for parliament and Bloemfontein for the Appellate Division, a compromise South Africa still keeps.</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Through early contact with the Philippines, words such as dalam hati, “sympathy”, luwalhati, “glory”, tengah hari, “midday”, and sedap, “delicious”, entered Tagalog and other Philippine languages, and the early settlement of a Cape Malay community in <b>Cape Town</b> brought many Classical Malay words into Afrikaans.</blockquote><a href="/language/malay">Malay · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
